  "id": "Compound::DB00755 - Gene::5914",
  "verbalization": "Tretinoin, a retinoid drug, is linked to Rara through a shared pathway involving Acitretin, a metabolite of Tretinoin. The evidence suggests that Tretinoin causes various clinical effects, including Hyperostosis, Decreased Night Vision, and Decreased Tolerance, all of which are mediated by Acitretin. Acitretin binds to Rara, a key component of the retinoic acid receptor, which is central to the biological effects of retinoids. The lowest common ancestor of these relationships is Alpha, Beta-Unsaturated Monocarboxylic Acid, indicating a common metabolic pathway. The cumulative scores from the three paths suggest a plausible mechanistic link between Tretinoin and Rara, particularly through the activation of the retinoic acid receptor pathway. The evidence supports the idea that Tretinoin's effects are mediated by Acitretin, which in turn interacts with Rara, though the exact role of Rara in the observed clinical effects remains to be fully elucidated.",
